p.src:
The dimensionful particle action is $A=-mc^2\int
\sqrt{1+\frac{2u}{c^2}
-\frac{\dot{\mathbf{x}}^{\,2}}{c^2}}\,dt.$ Both $u/c^2$ and $\dot{\mathbf{x}}^{\,2}/c^2$ are small. Keeping their first-order contributions, $\sqrt{1+\epsilon}
=1+\frac{\epsilon}{2}+O(\epsilon^2),
\qquad
\epsilon=\frac{2u-\dot{\mathbf{x}}^{\,2}}{c^2},$ gives $\begin{aligned}
L
&=-mc^2
\sqrt{1+\frac{2u-\dot{\mathbf{x}}^{\,2}}{c^2}}\\
&=-mc^2-mu+\frac{m}{2}\dot{\mathbf{x}}^{\,2}\\
&\quad+O\!\left(c^{-2}
 [u^2+u\dot{\mathbf{x}}^{\,2}+\dot{\mathbf{x}}^{\,4}]\right).
\end{aligned}$ The constant rest-energy term has no effect on the equations of motion. What remains is $L_{\mathrm{nr}}
=\frac{m}{2}\dot{\mathbf{x}}^{\,2}-m u(\mathbf{x}),$ and therefore $m\ddot{\mathbf{x}}=-m\boldsymbol{\nabla}u.$ The test mass cancels, as it must for universal free fall. The potential energy is $m u$, while the acceleration is independent of $m$.

