For $d\Omega=0$, define $a(r)=1-\frac{r_s}{r}.$ The radial metric and Lagrangian are $\begin{aligned}
d\tau^2&=a\,dt^2-\frac{dr^2}{a},\\
L&=-m\sqrt{a-\frac{\dot r^2}{a}},
\qquad
\dot r=\frac{dr}{dt}.
\end{aligned}$ Because $L$ has no explicit $t$-dependence, its Hamiltonian is conserved. The radial momentum is $p_r=\frac{\partial L}{\partial\dot r}
=\frac{m\dot r}{a\sqrt{a-\dot r^2/a}},$ and $\begin{aligned}
H
&=p_r\dot r-L\\
&=\frac{ma}{\sqrt{a-\dot r^2/a}}
=E.
\end{aligned}$ Solving for the coordinate velocity gives $\boxed{
\dot r^2
=a^2\left(1-\frac{m^2}{E^2}a\right).
}$ The board calculation tests several intermediate algebraic forms; the boxed expression is the consistent result.

